<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Binnenhof</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Binnenhof is a complex of buildings in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, that grew from the residence of the counts of Holland, begun by Count William II around 1250, with the great Hall of Knights (Ridderzaal) built under Floris V. Since the late 16th century it has housed the States General and other organs of government, and it remains the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/binnenhof.jpg" alt="Binnenhof" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Den_Haag_Binnenhof_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Little is known about the origin of the Binnenhof. Count Floris IV of Holland bought the grounds in 1229 from Dirk van Wassenaer and built a hunting lodge. His successor William II is often credited with its foundation after he became King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1248, and presumably began the Ridderzaal, a great hall for receiving guests. The hall was finished under Floris V, when the buildings were also walled in with a gate and the Hofkapel was built. The complex had a public part, including the Ridderzaal, and a private area for residence and private meetings.</p><p>After the House of Holland died out in 1299 the county passed to the counts of Hainaut, who barely resided there. Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, who reigned from 1358 to 1404, was the first to use it more or less permanently as his primary residence and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> to grow nearby. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-delft">Treaty of Delft</a> in 1428 brought the county into the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, whose stadtholders of Holland used the offices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> was proclaimed in 1581 the Ridderzaal was at first a public space used by traders, stallholders and booksellers. In 1584 stadtholder Maurice moved into the stadtholder's quarter, and in the same year the hall became the meeting place of the newly formed States General. Maurice's expansions began a gradual reconstruction that ended with the southern wing built under stadtholder William V in the late 18th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> first met there in March 1796 in William V's former ballroom, which housed the House of Representatives until 1992. Between 1806 and 1810, under French rule, the administrative centre moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and the Binnenhof was considered for demolition. It was threatened again in 1848, when the States General wished to demolish the old buildings symbolically, but local residents protested successfully. A full-scale renovation began in autumn 2021.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Gothic Ridderzaal, literally Knight's Hall, forms the centre of the complex, and on Prinsjesdag, the third Tuesday of September, the King gives the Speech from the Throne there. Other buildings form a rectangle around it, with a large courtyard in front and a smaller square behind. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stadtholder">Stadtholder</a>'s Gate dates from 1620. The Senate sits in the western corner, and the lower house now meets in the modern eastern part. The Prime Minister's office has been in the small northern tower, the Torentje, since 1982, and the cabinet meets in the Trêveszaal, built for negotiations during the Eighty Years' War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Binnenhof” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dirk III defeated an imperial army at Vlaardingen in 1018; William II was elected King of the Romans in 1247 and began the <b>Binnenhof</b> at The Hague.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
